# Updating Pontia

Pontia updates from the signed stable release channel. Check the installed version with:

```sh
pontia --version
```

## Update the local control plane

Run as the same user that owns the per-user service:

```sh
pontia update
```

The updater:

1. resolves the running installation directory;
2. acquires an update lock;
3. downloads the stable release manifest over HTTPS;
4. verifies its embedded Ed25519 signature;
5. verifies each artifact URL, size, and SHA-256 digest;
6. stages replacement `pontia` and `pontiad` binaries in the installation directory;
7. restarts a running managed service and checks health;
8. restores the previous binaries and attempts service recovery if replacement or restart fails.

`pontia` and `pontiad` must be regular files in the same directory, and the CLI must be running as the installed executable named `pontia`. A package-manager wrapper or renamed executable cannot self-update.

The Dashboard defaults to the release archive matching the binary version, so a successful update also moves the served Dashboard to that version unless `[dashboard].source` is overridden.

## Install a specific release

The installer accepts a release beginning with `v`:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://get.pontia.dev/install.sh | PONTIA_VERSION=v0.5.2 sh
```

This installs binaries but does not by itself update an existing service definition. Run `pontia up` afterward when changing installation locations or intentionally installing a specific version. Normal in-product updates follow only the stable channel and do not provide a downgrade command.

## Update a self-hosted Edge

Run on the Edge server:

```sh
sudo pontia-edge update
```

The Edge updater verifies the same signed release channel, replaces the managed `/usr/local/bin/pontia-edge`, restarts an active service, verifies that the new process remains stable, and rolls back on failure.

Initialization enables automatic Edge updates. Check or change them with:

```sh
sudo pontia-edge auto-update status
sudo pontia-edge auto-update enable
sudo pontia-edge auto-update disable
```

The timer checks hourly with up to ten minutes of random delay. Disabling the timer does not interrupt an update already running.

## Before and after updating

Pontia applies bundled local database migrations during normal startup when `run_migrations` is enabled. Do not copy a newer local database into an older release and assume it is compatible.

After an update, run:

```sh
pontia status
```

For an Edge, check `systemctl status pontia-edge.service` and its Cloud health badge. See [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) if automatic rollback or service recovery reports an error.
